WHAT IS TCL?
------------
TCL stands for: Transaction Control Language

TCL commands are used to manage transactions in a database.
They help control:
  • Saving changes
  • Undoing changes
  • Creating checkpoints

================================================================================
WHAT IS A TRANSACTION?
================================================================================

A Transaction is a group of SQL statements executed together as one unit.

Example:
  UPDATE Accounts SET balance = balance - 500 WHERE acc_no = 101;
  UPDATE Accounts SET balance = balance + 500 WHERE acc_no = 102;

Both queries together form one transaction.

================================================================================
TCL COMMANDS
================================================================================

Main TCL commands:
  1. COMMIT
  2. ROLLBACK
  3. SAVEPOINT
  4. SET TRANSACTION

================================================================================
1. COMMIT
================================================================================

Definition: COMMIT permanently saves changes in the database.

Syntax:
  COMMIT;

Example:
  UPDATE Employees SET salary = 50000 WHERE emp_id = 1;
  COMMIT;

Execution Flow:
  Step 1: Transaction starts
  Step 2: Data gets modified (salary updated)
  Step 3: COMMIT saves changes permanently

Diagram:
  Start Transaction
        ↓
  Modify Data
        ↓
  COMMIT
        ↓
  Changes Saved Permanently

Important: After COMMIT, changes cannot be undone.

================================================================================
2. ROLLBACK
================================================================================

Definition: ROLLBACK cancels changes and restores previous state.

Syntax:
  ROLLBACK;

Example:
  UPDATE Employees SET salary = 70000 WHERE emp_id = 2;
  ROLLBACK;

Execution Flow:
  Step 1: Transaction starts
  Step 2: Data changes temporarily
  Step 3: ROLLBACK removes changes

Diagram:
  Start Transaction
        ↓
  Modify Data
        ↓
  ROLLBACK
        ↓
  Changes Removed

Important: ROLLBACK works only before COMMIT.

Example with COMMIT and ROLLBACK:
  UPDATE Accounts SET balance = balance - 1000 WHERE acc_no = 101;
  UPDATE Accounts SET balance = balance + 1000 WHERE acc_no = 102;
  ROLLBACK;

Result: Money transfer cancelled. No changes saved.

================================================================================
3. SAVEPOINT
================================================================================

Definition: SAVEPOINT creates checkpoints inside a transaction.

Syntax:
  SAVEPOINT savepoint_name;

Example:
  UPDATE Employees SET salary = salary + 5000 WHERE emp_id = 1;
  SAVEPOINT sp1;
  UPDATE Employees SET salary = salary + 3000 WHERE emp_id = 2;

Rollback to SAVEPOINT:
  ROLLBACK TO sp1;

Meaning: Undo changes after savepoint only.

Diagram:
  Transaction Start
        ↓
  Update 1
        ↓
  SAVEPOINT sp1
        ↓
  Update 2
        ↓
  ROLLBACK TO sp1
        ↓
  Update 2 Cancelled

================================================================================
4. SET TRANSACTION
================================================================================

Definition: Controls transaction properties.

Example:
  SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY;

================================================================================
TCL EXECUTION ORDER (EXAMPLE)
================================================================================

Example:
  UPDATE Employees SET salary = 60000 WHERE emp_id = 1;
  SAVEPOINT sp1;
  DELETE FROM Employees WHERE emp_id = 5;
  ROLLBACK TO sp1;
  COMMIT;

Step-by-Step:
  Step 1: Update salary.
  Step 2: Create savepoint.
  Step 3: Delete employee.
  Step 4: ROLLBACK TO sp1 -> Delete operation cancelled.
  Step 5: COMMIT remaining changes.

================================================================================
TRANSACTION STATES
================================================================================

+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| State                | Meaning                     |
+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| Active               | Transaction running         |
| Partially Committed  | Waiting for commit          |
| Committed            | Changes saved               |
| Failed               | Error occurred              |
| Aborted              | Rolled back                 |
+----------------------+-----------------------------+

================================================================================
ACID PROPERTIES
================================================================================

Transactions follow ACID properties.

A → Atomicity:
  All operations succeed or none.

C → Consistency:
  Database remains valid.

I → Isolation:
  Transactions do not interfere.

D → Durability:
  Committed changes stay permanently.

ACID Diagram:
  Transaction
       ↓
  ACID Rules
       ↓
  Reliable Database Operations

================================================================================
AUTO COMMIT
================================================================================

Many databases use:
  AUTO COMMIT = ON
Each query commits automatically.

Disable Auto Commit:
  SET AUTOCOMMIT = 0;

================================================================================
DIFFERENCE BETWEEN COMMIT AND ROLLBACK
================================================================================

+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| COMMIT                   | ROLLBACK                 |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Saves changes            | Cancels changes          |
| Permanent                | Temporary undo           |
| Cannot undo after commit | Restores previous state  |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

================================================================================
DIFFERENCE BETWEEN DDL AND TCL
================================================================================

+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| DDL                      | TCL                      |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+
| Structure changes        | Transaction management   |
| CREATE, ALTER, DROP      | COMMIT, ROLLBACK         |
+--------------------------+--------------------------+

================================================================================
REAL-TIME EXAMPLE – BANKING SYSTEM
================================================================================

Transaction:
  Withdraw money
  Deposit money

Both must complete together. If failure occurs:
  ROLLBACK

SQL Transaction Flow Diagram:
  Start Transaction
        ↓
  Execute Queries
        ↓
  Error?
     ↓         ↓
   YES         NO
     ↓         ↓
  ROLLBACK   COMMIT

================================================================================
ADVANTAGES OF TCL
================================================================================

1. Data safety
2. Error recovery
3. Maintains consistency
4. Supports multi-user systems

================================================================================
DISADVANTAGES
================================================================================

1. Locks may slow performance
2. Long transactions consume resources

================================================================================
QUICK REVISION
================================================================================

+----------------+--------------------------------+
| Command        | Purpose                        |
+----------------+--------------------------------+
| COMMIT         | Save changes                   |
| ROLLBACK       | Undo changes                   |
| SAVEPOINT      | Create checkpoint              |
| SET TRANSACTION| Set transaction behavior       |
+----------------+--------------------------------+

Memory Trick:
  COMMIT    → Save
  ROLLBACK  → Undo
  SAVEPOINT → Checkpoint

================================================================================
INTERVIEW POINTS
================================================================================

1. TCL manages transactions.
2. COMMIT permanently saves changes.
3. ROLLBACK undoes changes.
4. SAVEPOINT creates checkpoints.
5. Transactions follow ACID properties.
6. COMMIT cannot be undone.
7. TCL ensures data consistency.
